     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Amlodipine 50 mg is five times the maximum daily dose, and I don't see a clinical reason for it. Confirming it doesn't make it safe for the patient, and you've told me you're not a clinician, so you can't act as the prescriber of record.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n\n- **If the intended dose was 5 mg or 10 mg:** tell me and I'll enter it right away. A prescriber should still verify it.\n- **If the treating clinician wants a higher or unusual dose:** they should enter or co-sign the order themselves and talk to pharmacy about it. I'd also expect the rationale and monitoring plan to be documented.\n- **If the patient's blood pressure is hard to control:** the medicine team can add or adjust other agents. Raising amlodipine past 10 mg isn't the usual approach.\n\nI'm glad to enter the order once the dose is corrected or a prescriber has taken responsibility for it."
      }
     ],
